course: dictionary
lesson: 912
title: Plants that catch animals
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Most plants make their own food from sunlight. A bladderwort is different: it is a carnivorous plant, which means it eats meat, or at least very small animals. It lives in ponds and bogs where the soil and water have very few nutrients, so it catches tiny creatures to get the extra food it needs.
explain: Read the clues in this sentence: «Bladderworts have no roots and float in ponds; their tiny underwater bags pop open and suck in water fleas.» A plant with no roots cannot pull food up from the ground, so it needs another way to feed. Its bladders are the little underwater bags on its stems, and they are its traps.
explain: Use the swap check. Put your own meaning in place of the hard word: «Bladderworts have tiny underwater bags that pop open and suck in water fleas.» That still makes sense, so you have understood it. Then read on, because the trap is very quick.
fact: A bladderwort is a meat-eating water plant.
fact: Its bladders are tiny bags under the water, on its stems.
fact: When a water flea touches the trigger hairs, the trapdoor opens and the flea is sucked in.
fact: The trap is one of the fastest movements in the plant world, taking about a thousandth of a second.
fact: Other meat-eating plants include the Venus flytrap and the sundew.
exercise: What do bladderworts catch to eat? | Pond weed | Stones | *Water fleas | Fish as big as your hand
exercise: Where does a bladderwort grow? | *In ponds, bogs and wet places | Inside caves under the ground | In deserts and dry sand | On the tops of tall trees
exercise: «Bladderworts have tiny underwater bladders that pump water out.» What is a bladder? | A kind of root | A type of flower | A pond animal | *A little bag
test: Why does a bladderwort need to catch animals? | It wants to keep the pond clean | *Its soil and water have very few nutrients, so it needs extra food | It likes the taste of stones | It is scared of frogs
test: «When a water flea touches the trigger hairs, the trapdoor opens.» What happens next? | The plant grows yellow flowers above the water | *The trapdoor opens and sucks the water flea in | The bladders pump water out of the plant | The plant floats up to the surface
exam: How fast does the bladderwort trap work? | An hour | A whole minute | *In about a thousandth of a second | About the blink of an eye
